ERROR REPORTS

Please send errors and suggestions to Frans Kaashoek and Robert Morris
(kaashoek,rtm@mit.edu).  The main purpose of xv6 is as a teaching
operating system for MIT's 6.1810, so we are more interested in
simplifications and clarifications than new features.

BUILDING AND RUNNING XV6

You will need a RISC-V "newlib" tool chain from
https://github.com/riscv/riscv-gnu-toolchain, and qemu compiled for
riscv64-softmmu.  Once they are installed, and in your shell
search path, you can run "make qemu".

THESIS PMFS PROTOTYPE (v3)
=========================

Current status, test evidence and limits: THESIS.md and
  docs/PMFS-REPAIR-REPORT.md          Chinese repair/test report
  docs/SEMANTICS.md                   exact supported subset and error semantics
  docs/QEMU-NVDIMM.md                 device and persistence-domain limits
  docs/XV6-LOG-RECOVERY.md            native xv6 log CI diagnosis and tests
  docs/EXCELLENCE-ROADMAP.md          original planning/baseline audit
  docs/LOCAL-SANDBOX-TESTS.md         historical pre-repair evidence

The kernel has an absolute /pm path adapter for ordinary file descriptors,
plus dedicated file/KV calls. v3 uses checksummed append-only data and COW
metadata, two checkpoints, explicit formatting, and a serialized writer.
It is a bounded teaching prototype, not a general VFS or full POSIX FS.

Unpatched MAP is ordinary guest RAM, NOT an NVDIMM. An experimental QEMU
9.2.0 patch supports one separately backed cold-plugged NVDIMM on virt and
advertises a private FDT node. See docs/QEMU-NVDIMM.md for build and limits.
The default cache-line touch is instrumentation, NOT hardware writeback.
Optional Zicbom emits CBO.CLEAN under explicit platform assumptions; QEMU
TCG does not simulate cache persistence. Process kills are not physical
power failures. v3 is incompatible with v2; there is no automatic migration.

  make qemu                 # volatile EMUL, a new empty PMFS each boot
  make kernel-pmem          # rebuild MAP with ordinary 160 MiB guest RAM
  make test-model           # bounded specification + mutation controls
  make test-xv6-crash      # test-only native log + orphan process-restart tests
  CPUS=3 ./test-xv6.py stress # concurrent native block-log writers and readback
  make test-recovery        # MAP restart/fault oracle; software boundaries
  make test-recovery-zicbom # same oracle, QEMU CPU with Zicbom enabled
  make test-recovery-nvdimm QEMU=/path/to/patched/qemu-system-riscv64
  make contract-full        # destructive legacy selftests on disposable data
  make contract-pmem2       # two-boot shared-RAM magic smoke, not power loss
  make bench-sweep          # matched operation counts; prints raw tick samples

IMPORTANT: kernel-pmem, contracts, benchmark and recovery targets (including
`make test-xv6-crash`) call make clean and rebuild fs.img. test-xv6.py can
also recreate fs.img. Run in a
disposable checkout and back up any data. Normal MAP startup refuses blank,
v2 or corrupted media without formatting it. Only when intentional, in the
guest run `pmformat --force` to destroy and format an unmounted PMFS image.
The recovery harness creates temporary disk/RAM images; its build still
recreates fs.img. Existing old thesis figures/PDF must not be submitted as
new performance or power-failure evidence.

Dependencies: RISC-V GCC/binutils, qemu-system-riscv64, Python 3 and bc.
